  doi = {10.1046/j.1365-2567.2000.00144.x},
  url = {https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1046/j.1365-2567.2000.00144.x},
  urldate = {2025-01-08},
  abstract = {Cell transmission from mother to offspring was demonstrated using mice with green ¯uorescent protein (GFP) transgenic markers. GFP transgene heterozygous (+/x) females were mated with GFP (x/x) males, and GFP+ cells in the GFP (x/x) fetuses generated between them were analysed to assess maternal blood cell transmission to conceptuses in utero. The GFP+ maternal cells were observed throughout the body of the fetuses, as shown by ¯uorescence stereomicroscopy. Cell entrance into the fetal immune system was shown by histochemical and ¯ow cytometric analyses of fetal organs such as thymus, spleen and liver. The GFP+ maternal cells persisted in the offspring until postpartum. Next, GFP (x/x) neonates fed by GFP+ foster mothers were examined to study the transfer of maternal milk leucocytes to offspring through breast-feeding. GFP+ leucocytes that had in®ltrated through the wall of the digestive tract were mainly localized in the livers of neonates. Their accumulation in the livers reached a maximum on days 5 or 6, and these cells became undetectable, as assessed by either histochemistry or ¯ow cytometry, after day 9 of starting foster nursing. Collectively, the present results demonstrate two independent pathways of maternal cell transmission to offspring: transplacental passage during pregnancy and breast-feeding after birth.},
  langid = {english},
